nce motion in the House  of Commons in a way that was going to  lead to a Jeremy Corbyn government I  don't think that is the right approach  but I do think there is a point about  saying whether the House of Commons  would support a strategy which is in  favor of No Deal I think there's a  question as to whether it would and with  John Bercow as Speaker whether there  would be a means of frustrating is so  which is which is why I think rather  than focusing on No Deal as as perhaps  some might want to do you we should try  to focus on how we can try to break the  impasse and I think there are a number  of candidates who are doing that and I  think that is sensible interesting  though that you haven't been swayed by  Michael Gough showing a little bit of  iron core in suggesting that you know he  would be prepared to delay brexit for a  year in order to get a delay in order to  get a deal done well I have a very  my opinion of Michael anyone who has sat  around the cabinet table in recent  months and heard the way in which he has  addressed these issues with great  honesty and great clarity he obviously  comes to it from the spective of having  led the vote leave campaign so he is a  committed brexit here so I'm very  impressed by Michael but as I as I say  Rory Stewart will have my vote in the  the first round whilst we have you  Donald Trump and coming to the United  Kingdom next week why did it need to be  a state visit well look I think it's  important that we have a good  relationship with the United States  there are most important security ally  Donald Trump as president we don't all  have to agree with everything he says or  does the style or the substance but it  is important that we have a good  relationship with the President of the  United States if we have a state visit  if that helps cement that good  relationship it's very important for us  it seems to me to ensure that the United  States is properly committed to NATO for  example with if that depends